    "about_title": "About & safety",
    "about_p1": "<strong>What this is.</strong> Traditions of the Mind is an offline reference to 68 figures — Buddhist philosophers and contemplative teachers, together with psychologists and scientists who approached the same questions from other directions. Each entry is a short, simplified note on what the figure is known for, meant as a starting point for study rather than a substitute for primary sources.",
    "about_h_not": "What this is not",
    "about_p_not": "Not therapy. Not a diagnosis. Not medical, psychiatric, or crisis care, and not a guide to meditation practice. The notes are brief summaries and leave out nuance and disagreement within each tradition. Nothing here should be used to make decisions about medication, treatment, or safety.",
    "about_h_struggling": "If you're struggling",
    "about_p_struggling": "If you're in crisis or thinking about suicide or self-harm, please contact a real person: a crisis line, a doctor, or someone you trust. In the US, call or text 988. Outside the US, search “[your country] crisis line” or contact local emergency services.",
    "about_h_data": "Your data",
    "about_p_data": "Your language and theme choices are stored only in your browser's local storage on this device. Nothing is sent to us or to anyone else.",
    "traditions_title": "Traditions of the Mind",
    "traditions_intro": "Buddhist philosophers and contemplative teachers, plus a few figures who bridge back into psychology (Skinner → experimental psychology, Freud → psychoanalysis, Kahneman → cognitive science, Nāgārjuna → Buddhist philosophy, Namkhai Norbu → the Dzogchen contemplative tradition). Read them side by side and a single question runs underneath all of it: how have different traditions understood the mind, consciousness, suffering, behavior, and transformation?",
